  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nAn upper low currently over the eastern Dakotas, on the northern\nlimb of the broader central CONUS upper trough, is expected to\ntrack southeast down the Missouri River and then into the Ozarks\nof MO and northern AR before tracking east toward Memphis and\npoints east on Thanksgiving Day. The forecast track of the upper \nlow has shifted farther south before it makes a turn to the east. \nThus, the system will impact the region a bit more than what was \nexpected 24 hours ago. Winds will pick up Wednesday night into \nThursday, especially in the terrain of NW AR and SE OK, on the \nback side of strengthening low pressure. In addition, the upper \nlow is basically expected to fall over on its side so to speak, \nallowing wrap-around moisture to penetrate farther south and west.\nThe latest CAMs (HRRR/RRFS) as well as the EC/UKMET bring the \npotential for some light snow into portions of NW AR on turkey \nday. Accumulations should remain light, and no impacts to travel\nare expected. The snow will taper off by evening as the upper low\nmoves away.\n\nA sharp warming trend is forecast into Friday, with even warmer\ntemps on Saturday as south winds return.\n\nThe UKMET and ECMWF bring the next shortwave trough across the\ncentral part of the country on Sunday. They both have a strong\narea of surface low pressure developing late Saturday and tracking\nover or nearby with a strong cold front sweeping across the \nregion into Sunday. The warm and windy conditions Saturday will\nraise fire weather concerns briefly. Given the progressive nature\nof the system and timing of moisture return ahead of it, the best\nchances for showers with the system will be over NW AR. Cooler \nweather is expected Sunday, though temps should be close to normal\nfor this time of year. The GFS does not show a system during this\ntime frame but is also not in agreement with the multi-model \nensemble means which show a wave moving through the area. Thus, \nits solution will be discounted for this forecast.\n\nLacy",
